Subject to this License, each Licensor grants You, for the duration of the applicable right, a
worldwide, royalty-free, non-exclusive license under copyright and copyright-like rights to run,
study, reproduce, modify, adapt, make derivative works of, display, perform, Convey, Provide, and
otherwise exercise those rights in its Contributor Version.

Each Contributor also grants a worldwide, royalty-free, non-exclusive patent license to make,
have made, use, offer, sell, import, run, modify, Convey, and Provide its Contributor Version
under Essential Patent Claims it controls now or later. An **Essential Patent Claim** is a claim
necessarily infringed by a licensed exercise of that Contributor Version, alone or in a
combination that Contributor supplied or designed or documented the Contributor Version to
support. It excludes claims infringed only by Your later Modification or unrelated combination.

The grants are irrevocable except as expressly provided in sections 10 and 11. Each downstream
Recipient receives rights directly under section 9; sublicensing is neither granted nor needed.

To the extent law permits, each Licensor agrees not to assert moral or similar rights solely to
prevent licensed conduct. No trademark, service mark, trade name, logo, publicity, or endorsement
right is granted, except accurate reproduction of required notices.

You may run, study, copy, and modify Covered Software without sections 3 through 9 while You do
not Provide it. You may engage agents acting only for You if they cannot use or disclose the work
independently.

A Sovereign User does not Convey, Provide, or Operate Covered Software merely by receiving public
or private packages or causing that user's browser, Kernel, controller, or other tool to compile,
assemble, install, store, run, or privately modify them for that User Deployment. That user need
not publish source or modifications; use a public repository; publish or retain package lists or
combined Wasm; calculate, verify, register, or disclose hashes or deployment records; investigate
another party's compliance; or operate a source service. This paragraph does not cover Providing
the work to another person or coordinated evasion. Communicating or exchanging User Data is not
Providing the software. Automated availability of runtime or package assets incidental to the
personal deployment is not Conveying unless the user affirmatively acts to distribute Covered Software.

## 3. Providing copies, source, executables, and network access

### 3.1 Duties for every copy

When You Convey Covered Software, You must provide it under this License, preserve accurate
copyright, authorship, patent, license, and warranty notices, supply this License and required
third-party notices, and identify any Modification with a reasonable description and date.

Every Modification and the work based on Covered Software as a whole must remain under this
License, subject only to true aggregates and section 8. You may charge for a copy or service, but
must not charge a Recipient again merely to exercise rights already granted.

### 3.2 Source and Executable Forms

When You Convey Source Form, it must include Complete Corresponding Source or a functioning
no-charge link to it.

When You Convey Executable Form, You must supply either:

1. Complete Corresponding Source with it; or
2. durable, no-charge, unrestricted network access to copy that source.

Network access must last while You Provide that version and for at least three years after the
last time You Provide it. A third-party host does not relieve Your responsibility. If You merge,
subdivide, or transfer substantially all relevant assets, You must transfer the source obligation
and use reasonable efforts to transfer all relevant source You hold. A successor that Provides the
affected version must honor that obligation and use reasonable efforts to obtain source held by
its predecessor.

A person that only forwards an intact package may pass through its included source or upstream
source offer without a separate hosting duty, unless that person knows the offer is materially false
or unavailable. After actual knowledge, stop Conveying, supply the source, or restore the offer. This
does not relieve the person whose own conduct created or controls the offer.

Covered Software in the source must be under this License; each independent component remains
under its own license. Source access must require no broader identification, contract, or payment
than executable access and must be reasonably usable with the supplied instructions. Functionally
equivalent builds need not be byte-identical, and a user's package selection, deployment identity,
prior state, or other personal context need not be disclosed.

### 3.3 Packages and browser assembly

When You Convey a package containing Covered Software, supply its Package Information. A packaging
tool may generate or supplement that information automatically.

A browser compiler or installer that You Provide must show the user the selected packages and
material compatibility or data-loss warnings before installation, but this License does not require
a package digest, compiler-output digest, installation-payload digest, installed-module hash, build
record, public registry, content-addressed store, or persistent on-canister record.

### 3.4 Installation and network operation

Complete Corresponding Source must include Installation Information. You need not reveal user or
shared infrastructure secrets, but if You cannot give a deployment-specific credential or an
equivalent independent install method, You may not Convey that Executable Form for a User
Deployment or represent the deployment as sovereign.

When You Operate Covered Software for a Recipient, that Recipient must have a prominent,
convenient, no-charge way to obtain the exact Complete Corresponding Source, this License,
third-party notices, and Your Modification notice throughout operation and for at least three
years afterward.

Source for installed or running code may not be embargoed. Exploit instructions, unpublished
vulnerability analysis, private keys, and unrelated security material are not Complete
Corresponding Source and may be handled through coordinated disclosure.

### 3.5 Structural deployment duty

Each Executable Form of a Deployment Component intended for User Deployments must implement the
part of the User Upgrade Path within its practical function and control, interoperate with the
documented remaining components, and introduce no deployment-specific third-party veto. Together,
the supplied components must support establishment of the complete path in section 5. These
conditions apply when the component is Conveyed even if the publisher never administers a
particular deployment.

## 4. Non-production development

You may Convey incomplete source or test builds of a Deployment Component that do not yet satisfy
sections 5 through 8 when they are conspicuously marked non-production, carry this License and
Complete Corresponding Source, and are supplied only for development, review, testing, archival
preservation, or good-faith security research.

This exception does not permit ordinary User Data, ordinary end-user operation, or a production
service. It covers work on the Covered Software itself and does not authorize offering its
ordinary functionality as a development, teaching, archival, or security product or service.

## 5. User Deployments and the User Upgrade Path

### 5.1 Personal scope

A User Deployment is for one Sovereign User. Guests, contacts, collaborators, and remote
counterparties may interact with Applications but must not receive a second independent tenant,
personal environment, administrative domain, or ownership interest inside that deployment.

The Sovereign User may use multiple personal profiles and may delegate limited actions without
creating another Sovereign User.

### 5.2 User Upgrade Path

A **User Upgrade Path** is a documented, effective sequence through the User Deployment or
Controller Authority under the Sovereign User's control that lets the user:

1. inspect the installed Kernel, packages, versions, licenses, available source, known User
   Authorizations, and Controller Authorities;
2. select a complete package set, including a Kernel package and any selected Application
   Packages, or complete Wasm satisfying the neutral Underlying Platform Service's documented
   module-validity requirements, chosen or built by the user, including modified, unsigned,
   unlisted, private, and differently governed inputs;
3. inspect material state-compatibility or data-loss warnings;
4. compile when needed and install or replace the complete Wasm without a deployment-specific
   third-party approval or secret; and
5. when desired, use the disclosed Self-Controller to establish a directly user-controlled
   external platform Controller Authority, and thereafter use documented platform tooling to add,
   remove, or reconfigure controllers without deployment-specific third-party approval.

A path may satisfy complete-Wasm replacement by compiling a complete package set under the user's
control. A separate raw-Wasm upload interface is not required if modified and unofficial package
sets can produce and install the complete Wasm without third-party approval. The provider need not
know or predetermine the resulting Wasm. Integrity checks and local build information are permitted
as product features but are not conditions of this License.

The safe in-product upgrade path may objectively enforce stable-memory compatibility, schema and migration
lineage, predecessor identity, checked journals, monotonic versions, dependency consistency, archive safety,
resource bounds, integrity, and authentic user approval. Rules must be app-independent and need no official
endorsement. A separately warned controller-level replacement may handle incompatible or destructive Wasm.

The User Upgrade Path must be documented and reasonably usable. Differences intrinsic to obtaining,
preparing, compiling, reviewing, or troubleshooting unofficial inputs do not violate this rule. The
Kernel or provider must not add a burden whose purpose or practical effect is to favor official inputs
and must not knowingly add, remove, substitute, or materially change software the user selected without
that user's informed authorization.

### 5.3 Readiness, activation, and handoff

A party must not enable ordinary use or storage of ordinary User Data in a Managed Deployment
until the Sovereign User has a User Upgrade Path.

A one-time activation credential may complete handoff if its holder can redeem it without further
provider action, redemption supplies the authority needed for the path, and the provider has
already retired every hidden or mandatory authority. Marketing labels such as preview,
provisioned, or complete do not delay this rule after ordinary use or storage begins.

Temporary bootstrap authority is permitted for creation, funding, installation, and
initialization. Any authority remaining after handoff must satisfy section 5.4.

### 5.4 Controllers and authority

The User Upgrade Path must provide a documented way to inspect Controller Authorities and User
Authorizations known or reasonably knowable to the party Providing the Deployment Component,
including indirect authority that party created, procured, used, arranged, or shares with an
affiliate or coordinated actor.

A Self-Controller is permitted while it acts only through user-authorized Kernel operations and
is not a third-party veto. The ordinary UI need not remove a Self-Controller required for the
checked self-upgrade path if it clearly explains that fact and lets the user add a directly
controlled external controller for platform-level management.

A third-party Controller Authority, or third-party User Authorization able to replace complete Wasm or alter
controllers, is permitted after specific, affirmative, default-off consent and a warning matched to its power.
Explain that a platform controller may replace Wasm or remove user authority and that full User Authorization
may direct the Kernel to do the same. After handoff, each must remain affirmatively retained and removable or
revocable through the User Upgrade Path. “Retained” does not require periodic reconfirmation.

Such a third-party authority present before this License applied may remain if it is disclosed,
warned about at the first reasonable interactive opportunity, and removable or revocable through
the User Upgrade Path. If the user declines it, remove or revoke it. No historical consent registry
or periodic reconfirmation is required.

A provider must not design or operate an authority it supplies, controls, coordinates, or requires to
remove, override, or materially impair the User Upgrade Path, even when the user selected it. An
exception applies on a contemporaneous authenticated user request or to the minimum necessary during
a documented active compromise, for the shortest feasible period and no more than seventy-two hours
without fresh, specific, freely given user authorization, followed by prompt notice and restoration.

You must not hide or require an authority, withhold a unique deployment gate, prevent the user
from establishing a direct Controller Authority or using it to remove or reconfigure another
authority, or make replacement depend on another controller's cooperation. This does not require
ordinary-interface removal of the disclosed Self-Controller allowed above.

### 5.5 Data, external dependencies, and user changes

Disclose any provider-controlled external service or credential essential to Kernel function and
the consequences of replacement. For provider-controlled state essential to continued Kernel
use, supply user-controlled credentials or a documented export or migration path.

Before a known destructive replacement, give a clear warning and a reasonable opportunity to use
any available backup or export. This License does not promise state compatibility with arbitrary
Wasm or require a backup facility that Covered Software does not otherwise provide.

No upstream party violates this License solely because the Sovereign User later changes
software, controllers, recovery, Applications, or configuration without that party's direction
or control. No Sovereign User loses private-use rights solely because of such a change.

## 6. Package governance and Daoization

A Governance Body may create, vote on, sign, publish, recommend, sell, support, or discontinue
its own Kernel, Application, package, repository, or Distribution. Its package governance does
not grant authority over a deployed user's computer.

**Daoization** occurs when a Governance Body or coordinated party imposes a deployment-specific
proposal, vote, signature, allowlist, co-signature, secret, or continuing service without which
the Sovereign User cannot use the User Upgrade Path. It also occurs when that party removes the
User Upgrade Path and substitutes governance-controlled whole-Wasm or controller decisions.

General governance of an Underlying Platform Service, optional reliance on recommendations, and
a user-selected additional controller are not Daoization while the User Upgrade Path remains
usable for current and future changes. Voluntarily asking for a vote does not make it required.

No permission is granted to Provide Covered Software in a Daoized Managed Deployment. Accurate
labeling does not create an exception. This rule covers anyone acting alone or together to cause,
procure, coordinate, or knowingly participate in the gated arrangement.

## 7. Kernel neutrality and Application equality

### 7.1 Capability equality

The Kernel must act as neutral infrastructure. General app-facing functions must be documented
Capabilities or narrow Kernel Administrative Components. A generally useful function for
Applications must be available to independently developed Similarly Situated Applications under
materially equal eligibility, interfaces, review, documentation, fees, admission rules, and
procedural burden.

Conditions must be necessary and proportionate to a documented app-independent constraint or
risk. They may meter resources, isolate code, reject unsafe behavior, require integrity and user
consent, and support bounded protocols or providers. They must not use identity as a proxy for
authorship, publisher, sponsor, signature source, registry listing, affiliation, governance, or
competition.

Equal terms do not guarantee simultaneous allocation of an exclusive or exhausted resource,
identical user consent, credentials, performance, or outcome. Scarcity must be handled through
published app-independent rules.

Registries, signatures, recommendations, and documented provider catalogs are permitted. A catalog
may use neutral security, privacy, and compatibility rules; an Application's maintainer listing,
signature, or affiliation must not gate an admitted general Capability. A discriminatory trust
policy is permitted only through informed user selection and must remain inspectable and changeable.

### 7.2 Ordinary and bundled Applications

Ordinary product functionality must remain in a separately identifiable Application, not the
Kernel. The Kernel Administrative Component exception is narrow and cannot absorb substantial
ordinary functionality or a material competing feature.

A Distribution may recommend or preinstall Applications. An Application selected during an
interactive install must be rejectable before activation. A starter Application installed before
handoff must be disclosed and removable, subject only to genuine, documented, technically
necessary, non-circular dependency order and data warnings, promptly after activation and before
it is required for ordinary use.

Bundled and maintainer-authored Applications must receive no hidden privilege or identity-based favoritism.
If the Kernel or Distribution gives an Application a default or exclusive role, that role must be
defined, inspectable, user-selectable, and replaceable by a compatible Similarly Situated Application;
omission of a role name cannot defeat replacement.

### 7.3 No Sherlocking or evasion

A Licensor of a Kernel may compete through an ordinary Application using the same rules. The
Kernel must not absorb a favored product or material competing feature, reserve a Capability, or
relabel product functionality as infrastructure.

Practical function and coordinated control govern. A helper, plugin, service, canister, library,
undocumented interface, or nominal Application remains subject to these duties only to the extent
it performs, receives, reserves, or gates a Kernel-level privilege or general app-facing function
in concert with a Kernel or Distribution maintainer. This limits Providing the combined system;
it does not claim copyright in independent code.

## 8. Applications, Combined Forms, and the assembly exception

**Combined Form** means an executable, package set, or Distribution containing Covered Software
and one or more separately identifiable Applications.

An independent Application does not become Covered Software solely because a Kernel reads its
manifest or documented interfaces; validates, compiles, links, wraps, stores, serves, or installs
it; generates neutral adapters; mediates Capabilities; or assembles it into one Combined Form.
The Application may keep its own license.

This **Application Assembly Exception** does not cover a Modification, Covered Software code or
templates copied into generated output, a Kernel Administrative Component, app-facing
infrastructure, or a nominal Application used to evade section 7.

When You Provide a Combined Form, preserve the available legal identity, notices, and licenses of
its components. Byte-level segmentation, prevention of compiler inlining, a reproducible component
mapping, and publication of a user's package selection or combined Wasm are not required. Supply
Complete Corresponding Source for Covered Software and source required by each independent
component's own license.

A restriction that applies solely to separately identified Application material is not an
additional restriction on rights in Covered Software. This exception grants no right under the
Application's license and does not make an otherwise incompatible combination lawful.

## 9. Downstream rights and prohibited additional restrictions

Each time You Provide Covered Software, every Recipient automatically receives the section 2
grants directly from every Licensor. You are responsible for Your conduct, not policing
independent Recipients.

You must not impose a license term, royalty for exercising granted rights, technical restriction,
withheld authorization, or other measure that limits a Recipient's licensed rights. You may
charge for copies, support, hosting, privacy-protecting services, indemnity, or warranty on Your
own behalf if the terms do not restrict source, the User Upgrade Path, or Kernel neutrality.

You may not invoke anti-circumvention law or a technological-protection claim to prevent licensed
installation, operation, inspection, or modification. To the extent You hold such power, You
waive it for those acts.

Independent contracts may govern unrelated services or grant more rights, but an ordinary
distributor cannot reduce rights under this License. A grant of additional permission by all
relevant Licensors remains allowed.

## 10. Patents

If You initiate a patent claim alleging that a licensed exercise of Covered Software infringes,
the section 2 patent grants to You terminate when the claim is filed. This does not apply where
the allegation is confined solely to Your additions, or to a defensive counterclaim, a request
for non-infringement or invalidity, participation required by law, or enforcement of this
License.

You may not knowingly Provide in reliance on a patent arrangement protecting only some
Recipients. Before Providing, extend equivalent protection automatically to all downstream
Recipients, obtain another lawful basis, or refrain from Providing.

If You knowingly Provide Covered Software whose licensed exercise necessarily depends on a
specific third-party patent not covered here, give the Recipient a prominent informational notice
identifying the claim and available licensing information.

## 11. Compliance, cure, termination, and enforcement

A **Material Violation** is a failure that materially denies source, downstream rights, the User
Upgrade Path, Kernel neutrality, required notices, or another condition of a grant. An
inadvertent immaterial defect promptly corrected is not a Material Violation.

For a first non-deliberate Material Violation, permissions continue during a thirty-day cure
period beginning when You receive written notice or obtain actual knowledge, whichever occurs
first. Cure restores full compliance and prevents termination.

A deliberate, concealed, repeated, or uncured Material Violation suspends permissions from the
affected Licensor for the affected Covered Software. When You cure, permissions are provisionally
reinstated. Reinstatement becomes permanent unless the Licensor gives final written termination
within sixty days after cure. A finally terminated party cannot obtain a new automatic license
for the same material without written reinstatement.

A temporary outage, active security incident, or platform-wide failure is not a Material
Violation for up to thirty days while You did not design or repeat it to deny rights, take prompt
reasonable steps to restore compliance, preserve every available independent fallback, and
accurately notify affected users. A provider-controlled condition beyond thirty days is not
protected. A genuine platform-wide impossibility outside Your control remains protected only
while You document restoration efforts and notify affected users at least every thirty days.
It never excuses source delivery, an intentional or repeated veto, or provider action past section 5.4's 72-hour limit.

During that protected period, sections 5 and 6 treat an otherwise compliant deployment as
retaining its User Upgrade Path solely for determining permissions under this License.

Termination never ends a license already received by a compliant downstream Recipient. You must
not use Your violation to disable or reclaim a Recipient's deployment or User Data.

Every affected Sovereign User and direct package Recipient is an intended beneficiary of source,
notice, installation, and sovereignty duties. An Application author materially denied equal
eligibility is an intended beneficiary of section 7. To the extent law permits, each may seek an
order for source, Installation Information, disclosure, restoration of the User Upgrade Path, or
equal access. This gives no ownership of another person's intellectual property, infrastructure,
secrets, or private data and creates no damages unavailable under law.

A Licensor's delay or failure to enforce is not a waiver.

## 12. Warranty and liability

“Protected Party” means a Licensor, Contributor, or compliant redistributor or operator that has
not separately assumed the relevant obligation in writing.

